# Auto-commit on delivery

When a feature, fix, or coherent set of related files is **done and delivered** (tests green if run, user-facing summary ready), create a git commit in the same turn **without waiting to be asked**.

This overrides the default “only commit when explicitly requested” habit for completed deliveries. Still do **not** push unless the user asks.

## When to commit

- A plan/todo set is fully completed
- A self-contained feature or fix is finished and reported
- Related files that form one logical change are ready together

## When not to commit

- Mid-task checkpoints unless the user asked for one
- Speculative or partial work still awaiting confirmation
- Empty trees / no meaningful changes
- Secrets (`.env`, credentials, `config/settings.yaml`, data dirs) — leave untracked

## How

Follow the repo git safety protocol: status + diff + log in parallel, draft a why-focused 1–2 sentence message, stage only intended files, commit via HEREDOC, verify with `git status`. Never amend unless the amend rules allow it; never `--no-verify`; never update git config.
